Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Regular expression to match markdown syntax
    std::regex markdownPattern(R"(^\*\*(.*?)\*\*)");

    // String to store the formatted output
    std::string html;

    // Use regular expression to replace markdown syntax with HTML tags
    std::regex_replace(markdown, markdownPattern, "<p>$1</p>");

    // Return the formatted HTML string
    return html;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to match the markdown syntax, which is enclosed in asterisks (`*`). The `std::regex_replace` function is used to replace the markdown syntax with HTML tags (`<p>$1</p>`). The function returns the resulting HTML string.

Here's an example usage:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "*This is a paragraph*";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    html = "<p>This is a paragraph</p>";
    return 0;
}
```

Note that this function only handles the basic markdown syntax. You may want to add more functionality to handle other markdown features as needed.

